	<h2>Enviornmental Concerns</h2>
			<p1>
			The impact that AI is having on productivity is undeniable. 
			You see it everywhere now and people are starting to use it
			as often as a traditional Google search. Their are positives 
			to the rise of AI but so far it is taking it’s tool on the
			environment. 
			</p1>
			<br>

			<p1>
				Training AI models alone take an immense amount of computer 
				power, a single ChatGPT prompt takes more than 5 times the 
				amount of electricity than a Google search takes. The other
				problem with this is that the models have short-shelf lives 
				therefore the energy used to train the current model will 
				essentially be wasted as soon as the new one comes out. The 
				amount of tera-watt hours used in 2026 is expected to be 1,050.
				Comparing the data centers to countries; that number would 
				make data centers the 5th largest consumer of electricity in 
				the world. 
			</p1>
			<br>

			<p1>
				The rise of Data Centers also is having a negative effect. 
				The amount of energy needed to run these center is so high 
				that they can cause grid instability. Due to this grid 
				operators use backup diesel generators in order to handle the 
				fluctuations. Inside of the data centers use chilled water to
				prevent the hardware from over heating, roughly 2 liters for 
				every kilowatt hour, which results in a strain on the local 
				water supply. Intensive mining is also used to obtain the raw
				materials need to make the GPU’s that are used inside of the
				data centers. Lastly, the impact of all of these raw materials
				and GPU’s being shipped all over the world is often overlooked
				and not talked about. The carbon footprint of this industry is 
				massive and most of the time companies don’t report detailed 
				data on water and energy use. 
			</p1>
				
